\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{Torsion is a coset of translations}
\date{2026-11-12}

\p{Let #{K} be a connected
\vocabk{finite cubical 2-complex}{fdip-006M}, and let each oriented
edge carry a pair #{g_e=(R_e,a_e)} from the group of
\ref{fdip-00OB}, with #{g_{\bar{e}}=g_e^{-1}}. A vertex gauge
assigns a pair #{h_x=(S_x,\xi_x)} to each vertex. It acts by
##{(hg)_e=h_{h(e)}\,g_e\,h_{s(e)}^{-1},}
as for the \vocabk{plaquette holonomy}{fdip-006N}.
The new pair has rotational component
#{R'=S_{h(e)}R_eS_{s(e)}^{-1}} and translational component
##{\xi_{h(e)}+\rho(S_{h(e)})a_e-\rho(R')\xi_{s(e)}.}
Along a closed path based at a vertex #{v}, the same cancellation
replaces the holonomy #{(R,b)} by its conjugate under #{h_v}. If
#{h_v=(S,\xi)} and #{R'=SRS^{-1}}, the conjugate has translational
component
##{b'=\rho(S)b+\bigl(\bbI -\rho(R')\bigr)\xi.}
A translation gauge, meaning #{S=1}, fixes #{R} and replaces #{b}
by #{b+\bigl(\bbI -\rho(R)\bigr)\xi}. The class of #{b}
in the quotient
##{V/\bigl(\bbI -\rho(R)\bigr)V}
is invariant under translation gauges. The quotient equals #{V}
exactly when #{\rho(R)=\bbI}. Every scalar unit has
that property, by \ref{fdip-00OB}, and the case #{R=-1} is
\ref{fdip-00OD}. A constant gauge #{(S,0)} sends #{b} to
#{\rho(S)b} and sends #{R} to #{SRS^{-1}}.}

\p{The plaquette holonomy is the holonomy of the attaching walk of a
square. Its translational component is the
torsion of that square, read as a class in the quotient of its
rotational component.}

\proof{
\p{The inverse in \ref{fdip-00OB} is
#{h_s^{-1}=\bigl(S_s^{-1},-\rho(S_s^{-1})\xi_s\bigr)}. The product
#{g_eh_s^{-1}} has rotational component #{R_eS_s^{-1}} and
translational component
#{a_e-\rho(R_eS_s^{-1})\xi_s}. Multiplying on the left by
#{h_h=(S_h,\xi_h)} gives rotational component
#{S_hR_eS_s^{-1}} and translational component
#{\xi_h+\rho(S_h)a_e-\rho(S_hR_eS_s^{-1})\xi_s}, which is the
displayed edge formula.}

\p{On a closed path the successive vertex elements cancel, as in the
path formula of \ref{fdip-006F}, and the holonomy is conjugated by
the value at the base vertex. The same multiplication with
#{h_v=(S,\xi)} gives rotational component #{SRS^{-1}} and
translational component
#{\rho(S)b+\xi-\rho(SRS^{-1})\xi}. For a translation gauge one has
#{S=1} and #{R'=R}, so #{b'} differs from #{b} by
#{\bigl(\bbI -\rho(R)\bigr)\xi}. The image of
#{\bbI -\rho(R)} is the zero submodule exactly when
#{\rho(R)=\bbI}, and the quotient is then #{V}. For a
constant gauge one has #{\xi=0}, and the formula reduces to
#{\rho(S)b} with rotational component #{SRS^{-1}}.}
}
